{"title":"FA MAS Contract Aware Recovery for Stateful Agent Service Workflows","paras":[
"Dhrumil Dutia, Independent Researcher, Seattle, Washington, USA",
"Abstract—Tool-using agents can leave duplicated, unauthorized or unresolved service effects despite plausible final answers. FA-MAS specifies contract-constrained recovery under latency, cost and state-risk limits. Its reference implementation enforces reduced gates and ranks finite recovery actions; full subgraph recomposition, calibrated diagnosis and multi-agent coordination remain proposed extensions. An original 52,500-row authored simulator uses unequal diagnosis parameters and a weaker terminal criterion, preventing causal interpretation of favorable safety contrasts. An initial 18,000-row matched exploratory study fails to demonstrate safety superiority. A further 18,000-row defect-correction study restricts compensation to existing uncertain effects, aligns selection and budgets, and counts detected unauthorized execution as unsafe. Corrected FA-MAS records 7.58% unsafe outcomes versus 9.67% without gates and 8.93% with first-feasible selection, but its difference from no-compensation is unresolved. These post-audit contrasts remain conditional on authored probabilities. Two local HTTP/SQLite implementations expose compatibility and cleanup behavior. An additional 150 direct-tool checks use 25 cancellation tasks from pinned public retail and airline code. After lost cancellation replies, reconciliation avoids repeated airline refund history; retail rejects repetition itself. These are reference-action mechanism checks, not conversational benchmark scores. A hash-locked clean dependency installation reproduces all simulator rows and public-tool traces. Development-only model-provider checks did not qualify a configuration for held-out analysis. The contribution is a recovery specification, executable mechanism evaluation and transparent limitations; no held-out live-model or production advantage is claimed.",
"",
"Index Terms—LLM agents, service composition, fault tolerance, service-level objectives, compensation, multi-agent systems, reliability.",
"I. INTRODUCTION",
"Tool-using agents are distributed systems. Their model calls, APIs, databases, queues, and human approvals have independent owners, schemas, latency distributions, permissions, and side effects. Conventional orchestration assumes that a failed step can often be retried. That assumption becomes unsafe when a timed-out payment actually committed, a syntactically valid response is semantically stale, or a substitute provider lacks the required authorization scope.",
"This paper asks: how should an agent runtime recover a stateful service composition while respecting end-to-end SLOs and preventing unsafe side effects? FA-MAS closes the loop from observation to contract checking, ranked failure belief, feasible recovery construction, constrained action selection, reconciliation, and post-recovery verification." ]},
{"title":"I INTRODUCTION CONTINUED","paras":[
"The paper contributes a formal contract-constrained recovery specification, a finite-action Pareto selector, two executable local service studies with terminal state judges, and a reproducible evaluation artifact. The specification includes a broader subgraph frontier, write-ahead/checkpoint protocol and multi-agent roles; these are not claimed as fully implemented. The measured contribution is mechanism behavior and disclosed failure cases, not universal performance improvement.",
"The novelty claim is intentionally narrow. FA-MAS is not presented as the first self-healing agent framework. Recent systems already detect abnormal trajectories, apply corrective prompting, or perform budgeted replanning [8], [9]. ReliabilityBench injects production-like tool faults [7]; failure-aware observability and AgentLocate address diagnosis and localization [10], [11]. FA-MAS instead studies their unresolved intersection with service contracts, SLO feasibility, and transactionally safe state recovery.",
"The evidence design separates mechanism validation from model capability. The reported simulator and HTTP/SQLite service conformance suites use controlled faults with known terminal state. A separate Tier 2 adapter harness supports development-only calls to pinned models in a simplified sandbox, but no configuration has qualified for held-out analysis. It does not exercise a complete multi-agent service composition, and its scripted integration runs are not live-model results.",
"II. BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK",
"Reasoning and tool use. ReAct interleaves reasoning traces and actions [2], while Toolformer learns when and how to call APIs [3]. AutoGen provides a programmable multi-agent conversation substrate [4], and LLMCompiler parallelizes dependent function calls [5]. These systems establish execution substrates but do not define recovery correctness for ambiguous state-changing calls.",
"Failure analysis and diagnosis. MAST identifies fourteen failure modes spanning specification, coordination, verification, and termination [6]. ReliabilityBench adds controlled perturbations and service faults [7]. Failure-aware observability diagnoses wasted computation from traces [10], while failure localization work attributes failures to agents and earliest decisive steps [11]. FA-MAS consumes comparable evidence but evaluates an executable recovery policy and service state.",
"Self-healing. Recent orchestrators map observed failures to targeted retry, replanning, and verification under bounded recovery budgets [8], [9]. These are direct comparators. Their existence makes a generic first-self-healing claim untenable. The remaining systems question is whether recovery decisions can respect explicit contracts and irreversible effects while meeting end-to-end budgets." ]},
{"title":"II RELATED WORK AND GAP","paras":[
"Service composition and transaction semantics. Service-oriented computing has long modeled composition, QoS-aware selection, and replacement. Sagas provide a foundational model for compensating long-lived transactions [12]. OpenAPI provides machine-readable interface descriptions [13], and OpenTelemetry standardizes traces and causal span relationships [14]. FA-MAS connects these service concepts to stochastic agent decisions and semantic contract violations.",
"Research gap. Existing lines address important fragments: tool selection chooses a service; observability records execution; localization attributes a failure; self-healing proposes a retry or replan; transactional middleware manages known application operations. Transactional-agent recovery is already addressed by SagaLLM [21] and Atomix [22]. We examine a narrower contract-enforcement and recovery-selection question; novelty cannot rest on introducing compensation, validation or durable agent state.",
"FA-MAS claims an integration and control contribution, not isolated novelty for contracts, tracing, SLOs, or compensation. The proposed technical center is a contract-valid, policy-authorized recovery frontier over success, latency, cost and risk. The executable selector currently prunes a finite set of single-action estimates; it does not enumerate executable recovery subgraphs. Hard gates reject infeasible actions before the executable Pareto selector prunes dominated alternatives.",
"Boundary of evidence. The prototype is an executable reference implementation. Its reported measurements establish internal consistency, paired comparison behavior, and safety–completion tradeoffs under known fault distributions. They cannot establish production readiness, model generality, or causal superiority on real services. Those claims require execution of the Tier 2 protocol in Section X.",
"Table I positions the closest families relative to the proposed mechanism."],"table":[["Family","Primary contribution","FA-MAS comparison focus"],["Tool agents","Action and API selection","State-safe runtime recovery"],["Reliability benchmarks","Perturbation and fault injection","Recovery mechanism"],["Observability/localization","Signals and attribution","Constrained action selection"],["Self-healing agents","Retry, verify, replan","Contracts and compensation"],["QoS composition","Provider selection","Agent semantic faults"],["Sagas","Compensating transactions","Proposed recovery ranking"]]},
{"title":"III SYSTEM MODEL AND OBJECTIVE","paras":[
"A workflow is a directed graph G=(V,E). Each node is an agent or service; each edge carries a typed message, control dependency, or state dependency. A run state s contains completed nodes, pending intents, observed outputs, external state snapshots, remaining budgets, authorization context, and the trace prefix. Every executable service i exposes contract C_i=(F_i,O_i,S_i,R_i,P_i,Q_i): functional, operational, state, recovery, policy/security, and quality clauses.",
"A failure hypothesis h=(class,node,time,outcome) belongs to infrastructure, protocol, semantic, agent, coordination, stateful-side-effect, or security classes. Given evidence e, the belief engine estimates b(h|e). Deterministic contract violations are evaluated before probabilistic diagnosis. The system never exposes private chain-of-thought; it stores observable actions, validator outputs, and evidence lineage.",
"A recovery plan rho is a finite subgraph containing zero or more reconciliation, compensation, retry, substitution, recomposition, validation, abstention, or escalation actions. Feasibility requires: all input and output contracts type-check; required scopes are available; state preconditions hold; uncompensated irreversible risk stays below policy; and predicted latency, cost, and attempt consumption fit remaining budgets.",
"Let p_succ(rho|s,e) be predicted completion probability; L, C, and R denote incremental latency, cost, and residual side-effect risk. FA-MAS selects arg max over feasible rho of p_succ - lambda_L L - lambda_C C - lambda_R R - lambda_V P(SLO violation). Hard policy constraints are not converted into soft penalties. A plan that crosses tenant boundaries or repeats an unresolved non-idempotent write is infeasible regardless of predicted utility.",
"Success requires both goal satisfaction and all terminal invariants. Safe termination is a separate outcome in which the runtime abstains or escalates without violating state or authorization. Acceptable outcome is the union of successful completion and safe termination. This distinction was frozen for the version 2 confirmatory simulator after the pilot exposed the construct-validity defect of treating safe refusal as uncontrolled failure.",
"The primary outcomes are task completion, acceptable outcome, SLO-attaining completion, and unsafe outcome. Unsafe outcome is the disjunction of duplicate side effect, unresolved invariant violation, or unauthorized attempt." ]},
{"title":"IV PROPOSED RECOMPOSITION FRONTIER","paras":[
"Algorithm 1 specifies proposed candidate-plan construction from the localized failure and current durable state. Candidate generation is deliberately broader than selection: retry is allowed only for read-only or idempotent operations, substitution requires an equivalent capability contract, and compensation requires a registered inverse or a governed manual-review path. Ambiguous writes first trigger outcome reconciliation.",
"Algorithm 1 RecompositionFrontier(s,e,B)\n1: H <- Diagnose(e,C,trace); choose calibrated top hypotheses\n2: A <- {reconcile,retry,repair,substitute,recompose,compensate,resume,abstain,escalate}\n3: generate bounded recovery subgraphs rooted at implicated nodes\n4: reject plans failing schema, precondition, scope, tenant, or approval checks\n5: reject unsafe retry when write outcome is unresolved\n6: estimate success, latency, cost, risk, and budget violation\n7: remove dominated plans and plans exceeding B\n8: return highest-utility plan; if frontier empty, abstain or escalate",
"Dominance is partial. Plan rho_1 dominates rho_2 when it is no worse in predicted success, latency, cost, and risk and strictly better in at least one dimension. This preserves alternatives for operators and sensitivity analysis. Deterministic policy checks precede prediction so a calibration error cannot authorize a forbidden action.",
"Recovery is receding-horizon. Only the next safe action is committed; new evidence updates the state and frontier. This matters when a reconciliation query discovers that an apparently failed write succeeded. The controller then validates or compensates rather than retrying.",
"Complexity. If k candidate actions expand to depth d, naive enumeration is O(k^d). These bounds describe the proposed subgraph algorithm, whose enumeration and memoization are not implemented. The executable finite-action selector uses pairwise dominance checks, taking O(n^2) time for n candidates, followed by linear utility ranking. Production implementations can replace the hand-calibrated predictor with a gradient-boosted model or contextual bandit while retaining the same hard constraints.",
"Correctness obligation. FA-MAS does not guarantee exactly-once execution for arbitrary external services. It guarantees only that its own controller will not intentionally issue a repeat of an unresolved non-idempotent intent without reconciliation or an explicit policy override. External providers must support idempotency keys, status lookup, compensation, or human escalation for stronger guarantees." ]},
{"title":"V ARCHITECTURE","paras":[
"The proposed runtime contains seven modules. The contract registry stores machine-checkable capabilities, schemas, pre/postconditions, state class, alternatives, compensation, scopes, and QoS profiles. The trace plane records model calls, tool calls, messages, validator evidence, state transitions, and causal parent identifiers using OpenTelemetry-compatible spans.",
"The failure-belief engine fuses deterministic validation, service telemetry, outcome reconciliation, verifier evidence, and optional model diagnosis. It emits ranked hypotheses with calibrated probabilities and observable evidence. The action generator compiles retry, repair, substitution, recomposition, compensation, abstention, and escalation into executable subgraphs.",
"The SLO selector applies hard feasibility gates and ranks the nondominated frontier. The durable state manager stores write-ahead intents, idempotency keys, checkpoints, provider outcomes, compensation status, and provenance. The policy gate enforces identity, scope, tenant, data boundary, approval, and recovery-attempt rules. The verifier checks terminal business invariants independently of final text.",
"Control flow. Before a state-changing call, the runtime records an intent and idempotency key. After a clear success, it records the provider outcome and advances the checkpoint. After an ambiguous timeout, it queries outcome state. If committed, it validates; if absent, it may safely retry; if partially committed, it compensates or escalates. After compensation, version 2 recomposes with an alternative and resumes rather than treating rollback as task completion.",
"Trust boundaries. Agent-generated arguments are untrusted. Contract validation and authorization occur outside the model. Service responses are also untrusted until schema, freshness, provenance, and business invariants pass. Recovery prompts cannot widen scopes or suppress deterministic failures.",
"Operational state is append-only for audit. Sensitive payloads may be hashed or redacted; lineage stores identifiers rather than raw secrets. Retention and access policy belong to the deployment rather than the model prompt."],"figure":"frontier.png","caption":"Fig. 1. Acceptable-outcome and safety frontier in the controlled simulator."},
{"title":"VI CONTRACT AND RECOVERY PROTOCOL","paras":[
"A contract manifest extends, rather than replaces, OpenAPI. Functional clauses describe schemas, preconditions, postconditions, and invariants. Operational clauses describe timeout, latency profile, availability, rate limit, freshness, and cost. State clauses classify read-only, idempotent-write, and non-idempotent-write behavior. Recovery clauses identify alternatives, checkpoint boundaries, reconciliation endpoints, and compensation. Security clauses identify scopes, tenant, data class, and approval requirements. Quality clauses identify confidence, provenance, and domain validators.",
"Example: refund. The refund operation requires a verified account, eligible policy decision, positive amount, refund:write scope, and a caller-supplied idempotency key. Its postcondition requires exactly one ledger entry linked to the case. A timeout with unknown provider outcome prohibits blind retry; the runtime queries refund status. If committed against an invalid case, it invokes reversal or escalates.",
"Example: reservation. Reserve is non-idempotent unless the provider honors the supplied key. The contract names inventory freshness, hold expiration, cancellation compensation, and payment dependency. A substitute provider is equivalent only if currency, traveler identity, cancellation policy, and authorization requirements match.",
"Protocol states are Ready, IntentRecorded, Invoked, OutcomeKnown, Ambiguous, Compensating, Reconciled, Resuming, Completed, SafelyTerminated, and Escalated. Every transition has a durable event. Crash recovery replays events and resumes from the last verified checkpoint.",
"Compensation is not erasure. It is a new state-changing action that can fail and must itself be traced and verified. A compensated workflow may resume with a substitute only when restored invariants and remaining budgets permit it.",
"Security faults differ from availability faults. An insufficient-scope candidate is rejected, not retried with broader credentials. A cross-tenant candidate is infeasible. Approval-required work transitions to safe termination or human escalation; it is never counted as an unsafe automatic success." ],"table":[["Contract class","Representative fields","Runtime decision"],["Functional","Schema, pre/postconditions","Repair or reject"],["Operational","Latency, cost, freshness","Retry or substitute"],["State","Idempotency, boundary","Reconcile first"],["Recovery","Alternative, compensation","Recompose or rollback"],["Security","Scope, tenant, approval","Reject or escalate"],["Quality","Provenance, verifier","Validate or abstain"]]},
{"title":"VII IMPLEMENTATION","paras":[
"The evaluated artifact is a Python reference simulator with a framework-independent trial interface. It instantiates the contract fields needed by the experiment: state class, compensation availability, authorization scopes, latency, cost, and substitute providers. The fuller manifest in Section VI is an architectural specification; fields not exercised by the simulator are not claimed as implemented. Policies encode static, retry-only, ReAct-style feedback, full replanning, budgeted self-healing, unconstrained dynamic composition, and FA-MAS. These baselines are controlled policy abstractions rather than line-by-line reproductions of external systems. The harness fixes task instances and injection seeds across systems, generating paired observations.",
"Three workflows are implemented. Refund retrieves an account, verifies policy, issues an idempotent refund, and notifies the user. Travel searches inventory, reserves, pays, and confirms; reservation models non-idempotent behavior and compensation. Incident response retrieves telemetry, localizes a service, executes reversible remediation, and validates recovery.",
"Fault injection selects a workflow step and mutates one of seven classes: infrastructure timeout or reset; malformed or drifted protocol; plausible semantic error; wrong agent action; dropped or duplicated coordination message; ambiguous or duplicated stateful side effect; and authorization or tenant-policy failure. A no-fault control measures steady-state overhead.",
"The version 2 simulator uses an explicit seed, 100 repetitions per stochastic cell, fault intensities 0.15 and 0.35, and common random numbers across architectures. Service latency is log-normal around contract-specific profiles; recovery probabilities are controlled parameters, not fitted claims about deployed models. Raw run rows preserve domain, fault, intensity, architecture, seed, action counts, latency, cost, detection, localization, recovery, completion, abstention, compensation, and invariant outcomes.",
"Tests assert deterministic replay, correct no-fault completion, security-gate behavior, and incident-domain execution. The experiment writes run-level CSV, grouped summaries, paired bootstrap confidence intervals, exact McNemar paired tests, Holm-adjusted p-values, ablations, figures, and an environment manifest.",
"Reproducibility. A single command installs the package and executes the frozen YAML configuration. The archive contains source, tests, configurations, raw observations, summaries, figures, and interpretation notes. The reported artifact hash should be generated at submission time after anonymization." ]},
{"title":"VIII EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN","paras":[
"Research questions. RQ1 asks whether contract-aware recovery improves completion and acceptable outcomes under controlled failures. RQ2 measures duplicate, inconsistent, or unauthorized side effects. RQ3 measures latency and cost overhead. RQ4 measures detection and localization. RQ5 examines adaptation and ablations. RQ6 defines the future live-model generality test.",
"Baselines. B0 is a static workflow. B1 retries with a fixed budget. B2 supplies tool errors to a ReAct-style controller. B3 replans from current state. B4 represents budgeted verifier-guided self-healing. B5 performs dynamic discovery and composition without contract or compensation constraints. B6 is full FA-MAS. All baselines share task instances, fault draws, and budgets; the simulator does not claim exact reproduction of external implementations.",
"Metrics. Completion requires terminal goal and invariants. Acceptable outcome adds policy-compliant safe termination. SLO attainment requires completion within 1,800 ms simulated latency, 18 cost units, and six attempts. Unsafe outcome includes duplicate side effect, invariant violation, or unauthorized attempt. Secondary metrics are detection, localization, recovery attempts, compensation, latency, and cost.",
"Statistics. Each paired comparison uses FA-MAS minus baseline on common domain-fault-intensity-seed tuples. We report paired bootstrap 95% confidence intervals from 3,000 resamples. Two-sided exact McNemar tests analyze discordant paired binary outcomes, and Holm correction controls family-wise error separately for each primary metric. Effect sizes and intervals receive priority over p-values. No run is removed as an outlier.",
"Ablations remove semantic-contract bonuses, compensation, constrained selection/substitution, provenance evidence, or dynamic substitution. Because simulator probabilities are authored parameters, ablations diagnose implementation behavior rather than discover natural causal effects.",
"Power and scope. With 4,500 paired full-system observations per baseline, intervals are narrow for aggregate simulator effects. This does not eliminate external-validity error. The experiment intentionally prioritizes exact ground truth and state inspection; the live-model protocol uses a smaller but independently labeled workload." ],"table":[["Factor","Levels"],["Domains","Refund, travel, incident"],["Fault classes","7 plus no-fault"],["Intensity","0.15, 0.35"],["Architectures","7"],["Repetitions","100 per stochastic cell"],["Full-system pairs","4,500 per baseline"],["All run rows","52,500 including ablations"]]},
{"title":"IX RESULTS","paras":[
"RQ1 Reliability. FA-MAS completes 85.0% of all runs and reaches an acceptable outcome in 88.4%. It exceeds static and retry-only completion by 9.3 and 2.9 percentage points, respectively. Against ReAct-style recovery the completion difference is +1.84 points, with a bootstrap interval of +1.02 to +2.64. FA-MAS completion is lower than full replanning (-0.96 points), self-healing (-1.58), and unconstrained composition (-1.29). Exact paired tests with Holm correction agree with the interval direction, but the practical interpretation rests on effect size and the explicitly simulated design rather than p-values.",
"Acceptable outcome changes the ranking because FA-MAS safely terminates 3.42% of runs rather than attempting unauthorized recovery. It exceeds all baselines on acceptable outcome: +12.76 points over static, +6.33 over retry, +5.27 over ReAct, +2.47 over replanning, +1.84 over self-healing, and +2.13 over unconstrained composition. All paired intervals exclude zero; Holm-adjusted exact McNemar p-values are below 0.025.",
"RQ2 Safety. FA-MAS unsafe outcomes are 4.87%, compared with 17.24% static, 11.89% retry, 11.02% ReAct, 9.84% replanning, 9.07% self-healing, and 9.27% unconstrained composition. Paired reductions range from 4.20 to 12.38 percentage points, and every 95% interval excludes zero.",
"These historical outcomes use unequal authored capabilities and a weaker terminal criterion. They do not isolate a constraint effect or establish safety superiority. The matched sensitivity result below is negative; raw completion also favors several simpler controls.",
"RQ3 Efficiency. Mean simulated latency is 783.6 ms for FA-MAS, near self-healing at 783.1 and unconstrained composition at 781.4, and above static at 706.8. Mean cost is 7.78 units, essentially equal to replanning and unconstrained composition and 0.31 above retry. No-fault overhead should be interpreted separately from recovery workload in a live deployment.",
"Figure 4 breaks down safety by fault class. The system-level advantage concentrates where contracts and state semantics can reject unsafe actions." ],"figure":"fault_safety.png","caption":"Fig. 4. Unsafe outcome rate by fault class and selected architecture."},
{"title":"IX RESULTS CONTINUED","paras":[
"RQ4 Diagnosis. Aggregate detection and localization are reported as implementation diagnostics, not claims about model-based root-cause analysis. Contract-sensitive faults receive deterministic evidence boosts in FA-MAS. Removing semantic-contract evidence increases unsafe outcomes from 5.21% to 6.57% within faulty FA-MAS cells, although completion rises from 83.95% to 84.67%. This is another conservative-safety tradeoff.",
"RQ5 Ablations. Fixed rules and no substitution both reduce completion to 81.57% and increase unsafe outcomes to 5.88%. Removing provenance leaves completion unchanged in this simulator but reduces localization, demonstrating that the current recovery policy underuses localization confidence. This null result is disclosed rather than interpreted as evidence that provenance is unnecessary.",
"The no-compensation ablation completes 87.95% versus 83.95% for full FA-MAS in faulty cells, with unsafe outcomes of 5.38% versus 5.21%. Even after adding compensate-reconcile-recompose-resume, compensation consumes budget and sometimes fails before alternate completion. Under the original judge this difference is small; the stricter matched study reverses the safety ranking. Compensation is not demonstrated to improve aggregate safety in this simulator. A production selector should estimate downstream resume value and use compensation only when unresolved state risk dominates.",
"Sensitivity. The two injected intensities control whether the selected fault becomes active. Because one target step is selected per run, intensity changes fault incidence rather than compound-failure depth. The manuscript therefore avoids claims about correlated cascading failures.",
"Negative findings matter. FA-MAS does not dominate every baseline on every metric. Its contribution is a controllable safety–completion frontier with auditable semantics. Applications can set risk weights and escalation policies according to domain cost rather than maximizing completion blindly.",
"Internal validity checks include paired seeds, deterministic replay tests, full retention of failures, and direct end-state validation. The complete run table permits independent recomputation." ],"figure":"ablations.png","caption":"Fig. 3. Unsafe outcomes for full FA-MAS and required ablations in faulty cells."},
{"title":"X LIVE MODEL CONFIRMATORY PROTOCOL","paras":[
"The simulator and local services are insufficient for claims about LLM agents. The Tier 2 harness includes OpenAI-compatible adapters, a held-out task bank, a simplified state judge, protocol hashing, and a live-only analysis gate. Its current model interface requests one recovery decision; it is not an end-to-end multi-agent execution environment. No model calls have been executed because exact identifiers and endpoints remain unpinned. Its future completion would supply additional evidence only within the implemented task and service semantics.",
"Models. Use at least one open-weight and one hosted tool-calling model, pin exact model identifiers and access dates, temperature zero plus a stochastic temperature condition, and cache non-sensitive responses where licenses permit. Do not tune prompts on confirmatory tasks.",
"Tasks. The frozen bank contains 30 confirmatory templates per domain with entity sets disjoint from three development templates. Run the five selected policies on identical task-fault-model-temperature combinations. Local transactional services represent refunds, bookings, and remediation so terminal state is inspectable without real money or infrastructure changes.",
"Labels. Deterministic state validators define completion and safety. Two blinded reviewers label semantic correctness and failure localization on a stratified sample; disagreements are adjudicated. Report inter-rater agreement. LLM judges may provide secondary labels but cannot define the primary outcome.",
"Faults. Inject timeouts, rate limits, malformed JSON, missing fields, schema drift, stale values, wrong arguments, dropped messages, ambiguous committed writes, duplicate deliveries, insufficient scopes, and approval requirements. Include compound faults only as a separate prespecified analysis.",
"Analysis. Fit mixed-effects logistic models for completion, acceptable outcome, and unsafe outcome with architecture as fixed effect and task template and model as random intercepts. Report marginal effects, paired bootstrap intervals, Holm-adjusted primary comparisons, latency survival curves, and cost–reliability frontiers. Analyze failures by class and publish all exclusions.",
"Stopping and changes. Freeze code and protocol before confirmatory execution. Correct implementation bugs only with a versioned amendment and rerun all architectures. Do not alter primary metrics after observing outcomes. Archive prompts, manifests, traces, checksums, and environment locks." ]},
{"title":"XI DISCUSSION","paras":[
"The results illustrate why agent reliability is a services-computing problem. Completion-only evaluation rewards a system that takes an unauthorized or state-unsafe action if the final text looks correct. By separating completion, safe termination, and unsafe outcome, FA-MAS makes policy choices visible.",
"Contracts are useful when they constrain behavior, not merely document APIs. Idempotency and reconciliation determine whether retry is legal; scope and tenant determine whether substitution is feasible; compensation determines whether a partially completed workflow can return to a safe checkpoint; latency and cost determine whether any recovery remains within the end-to-end SLO.",
"The recomposition frontier offers a practical division of labor. Deterministic mechanisms enforce schemas, permissions, and known invariants. Statistical models estimate uncertain success, latency, and semantic risk. LLMs may propose candidates or explanations but cannot bypass hard gates.",
"Tradeoffs. Conservative policies increase safe termination and may reduce raw completion. Compensation itself can consume latency, cost, and failure probability. Contract completeness adds engineering burden, and incorrect contracts can create false confidence. Providers without reconciliation or compensation require manual escalation.",
"Threats to validity. Internal threats include authored simulator probabilities, simplified service distributions, and baseline abstractions. Construct threats include the chosen acceptable-outcome definition and cost units. The three HTTP domain labels use the same service state machine; the workflow faults and policy rules were authored together. External threats include single-fault runs, absence of live model behavior, and simplified authorization. Statistical intervals quantify simulator sampling uncertainty, not model misspecification; deterministic conformance counts have no inferential p-values.",
"Mitigations are explicit paired designs, end-state ground truth, negative-result reporting, open artifacts, and the Tier 2 protocol. Claims remain limited to the implemented environment until that protocol is executed.",
"Ethics and security. The workflows use synthetic identities and local state. No real refunds, bookings, or infrastructure changes occur. Logs should minimize sensitive payloads. Human escalation must expose evidence and recommended actions without revealing private reasoning traces." ]},
